Homepage Structure
Design and implement the homepage with the following sections. You may refine the order if it improves the user journey.
Section A: Navigation
Create a polished, responsive navigation bar featuring:
	•	SK Energy logo. 
	•	Services dropdown. 
	•	How It Works. 
	•	Our Work. 
	•	About. 
	•	News or Insights. 
	•	A prominent primary CTA: "Get a Free Consultation" or an equally effective alternative. 
The navigation should feel lightweight and premium.
Make the header sticky if it improves usability. Ensure dropdowns work on desktop and mobile, have accessible focus states, and do not obstruct content.
Section B: Hero — The Most Important Section
The first screen must immediately communicate what SK Energy does, who it serves, and why visitors should trust it.
Create a compelling hero with a strong headline, supporting copy, primary and secondary CTAs, and high-quality imagery or a carefully composed image layout.
Explore messaging in this direction:
Headline: "Your energy. Better designed."
Supporting copy: "Solar, battery storage, low-carbon heating and EV charging, designed around your property and installed by one experienced team across central Scotland."
Primary CTA: "Plan Your Energy System"
Secondary CTA: "Explore Our Work"
Include a concise trust indicator beneath the CTAs, using verified credentials or business information already present in the project.
The hero must feel visually memorable, calm, confident, and premium.
Do not overload it with multiple competing messages, excessive badges, or unnecessary animations.
The first viewport should establish a clear hierarchy: headline, supporting copy, primary CTA, and visual proof.
Section C: Trust and Credibility
Create a restrained trust section using verified credentials, certifications, customer feedback, and business statistics where available.
Potential proof points from the existing website include:
	•	More than 12 years of experience. 
	•	Over 1,400 systems commissioned. 
	•	Five integrated renewable energy service categories. 
	•	MCS certification and other relevant accreditations. 
Audit all claims before using them. Do not invent certifications, statistics, awards, or customer ratings. Do not imply that an accreditation covers services beyond its actual scope.
Use clean typography and a subtle layout instead of a cluttered strip of logos.
Section D: Introduction to SK Energy
Introduce the company and explain its approach.
Suggested heading: "Good energy starts with a better plan."
Suggested supporting message: "Every property is different. We assess how you use energy, what your building needs, and what you want to achieve before recommending a system."
Highlight the company's integrated approach:
	•	One team from survey through installation. 
	•	Solutions designed around actual requirements. 
	•	Clear specifications and costs. 
	•	Long-term maintenance and aftercare. 
Use a strong editorial image alongside concise, benefit-focused copy.
Section E: Services
Create a visually engaging services section that makes it easy for different visitors to find the right solution.
Include five main service categories:
	1.	Residential Solar & Battery Storage Generate electricity, store surplus energy, and make better use of the power your home produces. 
	2.	Air Source Heat Pumps Improve home heating with a properly designed low-carbon system tailored to the property. 
	3.	EV Charging Install smart home, workplace, and fleet charging solutions. 
	4.	Commercial Energy Solutions Help businesses and commercial property owners explore solar, storage, and charging systems designed around site requirements. 
	5.	New-Build Energy Systems Coordinate renewable technologies with developers, construction teams, and build programmes. 
Use a considered card grid or editorial layout with strong imagery, clear titles, short descriptions, and meaningful links.
Each card should have a subtle hover treatment and a clear visual relationship to its corresponding service.
Do not make every card look identical if a more varied layout improves the design.
Section F: How It Works
Explain the customer journey in four simple stages:
01 — Initial Consultation Understand the property, energy usage, priorities, and budget.
02 — Survey and System Design Assess the building and develop a technically appropriate solution with transparent costs.
03 — Professional Installation Coordinate and complete the installation with attention to safety, quality, and disruption.
04 — Monitoring and Aftercare Help the customer understand system performance and access ongoing support.
Present these stages in an elegant horizontal timeline on desktop and a clean vertical sequence on mobile.
Reassure customers that recommendations should be based on suitability and evidence, not aggressive sales tactics.
Section G: Featured Projects and Case Studies
Create a portfolio section that demonstrates the quality and range of SK Energy's work.
Use existing case studies where their details can be verified, including examples of residential retrofits, commercial installations, and new-build projects.
Each project should include:
	•	A strong project photograph. 
	•	Project type and location. 
	•	The customer's challenge. 
	•	The solution installed. 
	•	Verified performance or financial results, where available. 
	•	A link to read the full case study. 
Use large imagery, thoughtful typography, and an editorial composition.
Avoid fabricated project photography, savings figures, payback periods, or testimonials. Clearly distinguish illustrative estimates from measured results.
Section H: Why Choose SK Energy?
Build a differentiated section that explains why customers should choose SK Energy over a conventional installer.
Focus on:
	•	Integrated expertise across multiple technologies. 
	•	Honest, property-specific recommendations. 
	•	Transparent system design and costs. 
	•	Professional installation. 
	•	Local knowledge across central Scotland. 
	•	Continued support after installation. 
Use concise, credible messaging and supporting visual elements.
Avoid empty phrases such as "industry-leading solutions" unless supported by real evidence.
Section I: Customer Testimonials
Display genuine customer testimonials from verified existing sources, if available.
Include the customer's name or approved attribution, the type of installation, and the relevant feedback.
Use an elegant editorial testimonial layout, not an oversized generic carousel by default.
If there is insufficient verified testimonial content, build a reusable section that can be populated later rather than inventing customer quotations.
Section J: Commercial and New-Build Callout
Create a dedicated section for business owners, commercial property operators, and property developers.
Suggested heading: "Building for the future? Get energy designed in from the start."
Explain how SK Energy can coordinate solar, battery storage, heat pumps, and EV charging with commercial requirements and construction schedules.
Include a clear CTA: "Discuss Your Project"
This section should look visually distinct from the residential sections while remaining consistent with the brand.
Section K: Frequently Asked Questions
Create a concise, accessible FAQ accordion that addresses genuine customer concerns, such as:
	•	Which renewable energy system is right for my property? 
	•	Can solar panels work with battery storage? 
	•	How does an air source heat pump installation work? 
	•	Do you install EV chargers for homes and businesses? 
	•	What affects the cost of an installation? 
	•	Which areas do you cover? 
	•	What support is available after installation? 
Answers should be useful, accurate, and based on verified company information. Avoid guaranteeing savings, suitability, installation timelines, or grant eligibility.
Ensure accordion controls work with a keyboard and communicate their expanded state to assistive technology.
Section L: Conversion-Focused Contact Section
Create a strong final CTA section that encourages visitors to take the next step.
Suggested heading: "Let's work out what makes sense for your property."
Supporting copy: "Tell us a little about your project. We'll help you understand the options worth exploring."
Include a simple, attractive enquiry form with relevant fields:
	•	Name. 
	•	Email address. 
	•	Phone number, optional where appropriate. 
	•	Postcode. 
	•	Customer type: Homeowner, Business, or Developer. 
	•	Service of interest. 
	•	Optional project details. 
CTA: "Request a Consultation"
Provide the verified company email as an alternative contact method.
Form requirements:
	•	Client-side and server-side validation where the existing backend supports it. 
	•	Clear labels and accessible error messages. 
	•	Loading, success, and failure states. 
	•	Spam protection where appropriate. 
	•	Appropriate consent and privacy wording. 
	•	No fake success messages or non-functional submission buttons. 
If no backend or submission integration exists, implement a clearly documented integration point and explain what is required to make submissions operational. Never claim enquiries have been sent when they have not.
Section M: Footer
Create a polished footer containing:
	•	Logo and concise brand statement. 
	•	Service links. 
	•	Company navigation. 
	•	Contact details. 
	•	Verified business address. 
	•	Relevant certifications, where appropriate. 
	•	Privacy policy and terms links. 
	•	Copyright notice. 
Ensure every link points to a real, appropriate destination.
5. Copywriting and Conversion Strategy
Write polished, original website copy throughout the homepage.
The messaging should be:
	•	Clear rather than overly technical. 
	•	Benefit-led without making unrealistic promises. 
	•	Specific rather than generic. 
	•	Trustworthy rather than aggressively sales-driven. 
	•	Appropriate for both residential and commercial audiences. 
Emphasise customer outcomes such as comfort, informed investment decisions, efficient energy use, better visibility of energy consumption, and a well-integrated system.
Use strong headlines, concise supporting paragraphs, clear CTAs, and scannable sections.
Do not promise guaranteed savings, energy independence, fixed payback periods, or specific environmental outcomes without supporting evidence.
Use British English spelling and terminology throughout.
